    <article class="version" id="v2.6.0">
      <div class="version-header">
        <a class="version-tag" href="#v2.6.0">v2.6.0</a>
        <span class="version-date">Oct 28, 2026</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/releases/tag/v2.6.0" class="version-link" target="_blank">Release notes &rarr;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="release-kicker">Faster to read, faster to drive</p>
      <p class="release-intro">
        <strong>A command palette opens anywhere with Cmd/Ctrl+K</strong>, the query editor completes and checks queries as you type, and the results table stays readable on wide sources. There are no database migrations and no config changes.
      </p>

      <ul class="release-highlights">
        <li><strong>Go anywhere, query anything</strong>Pages, sources across teams, saved queries, dashboards and alerts in one list. <code>@team/source status&gt;=500 last:1h</code> runs a search in any source.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask Chef from the palette</strong>Type a question and press Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+Enter. The Explorer opens on the scope and the answer arrives in the drawer.</li>
        <li><strong>An editor that helps</strong>LogchefQL errors are underlined as you type, and every field suggests values with counts.</li>
        <li><strong>More rows on screen</strong>13 rows instead of 3 on a 1280x720 laptop, with full timestamps and red or amber badges on failed and pending outcomes.</li>
      </ul>

      {unreleasedShots.map(shot => (
        <figure class="release-shot">
          <picture class="shot-light">
            <source srcset={`/screenshots/${shot.name}-light.avif`} type="image/avif" />
            <source srcset={`/screenshots/${shot.name}-light.webp`} type="image/webp" />
            <img src={`/screenshots/${shot.name}-light.png`} alt={shot.alt} width={shot.width} height={shot.height} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
          <picture class="shot-dark">
            <source srcset={`/screenshots/${shot.name}-dark.avif`} type="image/avif" />
            <source srcset={`/screenshots/${shot.name}-dark.webp`} type="image/webp" />
            <img src={`/screenshots/${shot.name}-dark.png`} alt={shot.alt} width={shot.width} height={shot.height} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
          <figcaption set:html={shot.caption} />
        </figure>
      ))}

      <aside class="upgrade-note">
        <strong>Before upgrading:</strong> there are no database migrations. A request for a missing file under <code>/assets/</code> now returns 404 instead of <code>index.html</code>. Hashed assets are cached for a year and <code>index.html</code> revalidates on every load, so an open tab reloads once after an upgrade.
      </aside>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type added">Added</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Command palette</strong>: Cmd/Ctrl+K on every page, including inside the query editor. Go to any page you can open, Add Source, sources across teams, saved queries, dashboards, alerts and collections, and run actions such as theme, timezone, copy link and sign out.</li>
          <li><strong>Scoped searches</strong>: <code>@team/source status&gt;=500 last:1h</code> gets team, source and range completion and opens the Explorer with the query running. On the Explorer, a complete filter over the current source's fields needs no <code>@</code>. Other text searches the logs as a literal (Cmd/Ctrl+Enter).</li>
          <li><strong>Ask Chef from the palette</strong>: Type a question, or start with <code>?</code>, and press Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+Enter. The same scope continues the conversation, and alert rows can start an investigation. Hidden when Ask Chef is disabled.</li>
          <li><strong>Checks as you type</strong>: LogchefQL errors are underlined, unknown fields get a warning, and hovering a field shows its type and top values.</li>
          <li><strong>Value suggestions for every field</strong>, with counts. Low-cardinality fields load while you type, others on Ctrl+Space. Lookups are cached, bounded and cancelled after 1.5 seconds.</li>
          <li><strong>Outcome badges</strong>: Failed and pending values in outcome-like columns get a red or amber badge. Success stays plain.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type changed">Changed</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>More rows</strong>: 13 instead of 3 at 1280x720, and 19 instead of 8 at 1440x900. The query controls fit on one row and the histogram is shorter.</li>
          <li><strong>Time controls</strong>: The timezone is a visible Local / UTC switch that also works in SQL mode and live tail. The time range picker locks, with a reason, while SQL or live tail controls the range.</li>
          <li><strong>Sort-key tip</strong>: It appears beside the query time, only while the query filters on none of the table's sort-key fields.</li>
          <li><strong>SQL completion</strong>: One provider, so keywords no longer appear twice. Columns come first after <code>SELECT</code> and <code>WHERE</code>, and values are suggested inside string literals.</li>
          <li><strong>Results table</strong>: Full timestamps at the column's precision, <code>Date</code> values without a timezone shift, widths that follow the content, readable fields before opaque identifiers, and identifiers that truncate in the middle.</li>
          <li><strong>Source inspection</strong> leads with recent activity and storage totals.</li>
          <li><strong>Admin pages and source forms</strong> share one layout with one primary action each.</li>
          <li><strong>Smaller pages</strong>: Pages without a query editor no longer download the editor bundle. The Teams page loads 742 KB of JavaScript instead of 5,732 KB, and the build output is 7.5 MB instead of 19 MB.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type fixed">Fixed</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li>The timezone switch had no effect in SQL mode and live tail, and the time range picker could change the range while SQL or live tail owned it.</li>
          <li>Copying <code>CREATE TABLE</code> failed on plain-HTTP deployments, and source activity offered a Retry that could not succeed when the sort key does not start with the timestamp.</li>
          <li>Column headers truncated short column names, and several icon buttons had no accessible name.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="version" id="v2.5.0">
      <div class="version-header">
        <a class="version-tag" href="#v2.5.0">v2.5.0</a>
        <span class="version-date">Oct 23, 2026</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/releases/tag/v2.5.0" class="version-link" target="_blank">Release notes &rarr;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="release-intro">
        This release fixes Explorer navigation, hardens security and makes alerts and exports reliable. It adds few features. Metadata migrations run automatically at startup: SQLite to version 38 and Postgres to version 12. Back up the metadata database before upgrading. On SQLite, sessions stored with a non-UTC timestamp are revoked, so some users must sign in again.
      </p>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type breaking">Upgrade notes</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Production config sample</strong>: The shipped <code>config.toml</code> sets <code>secure_cookie = true</code>, leaves OIDC commented out and leaves <code>api_token_secret</code> empty. Logchef refuses to start until you set the secret. Set <code>secure_cookie = false</code> if you serve Logchef over plain HTTP.</li>
          <li><strong>Webhook destinations</strong>: Alert webhooks reject private, loopback, link-local, CGNAT and IPv6 translation addresses by default and do not follow redirects. Allow internal receivers with <code>alerts.webhook_allowed_hosts</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Metrics</strong>: The <code>user_email</code> label is removed. Update dashboards that group by it.</li>
          <li><strong>Startup</strong>: A <code>--config</code> file that Logchef cannot read stops startup instead of falling back to defaults.</li>
          <li><strong>Frontend URL</strong>: Only a signed-in session can change it in the Admin UI, not an API token. Base URLs must be absolute <code>http</code> or <code>https</code> URLs.</li>
          <li><strong>Log fields</strong>: Request, <code>request error</code> and <code>panic recovered</code> logs use a redacted <code>route</code> template instead of the raw <code>path</code>. Update dashboards and alerts that filter on <code>path</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Query templates</strong>: Blocks without variables, such as <code>[[ AND active = true ]]</code>, keep their brackets so SQL arrays survive. Remove the brackets or add a variable to such queries.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type added">Added</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Export recovery per replica</strong>: <code>export.instance_id</code> lets Logchef recover a replica's interrupted exports at once instead of waiting for the age-based fallback.</li>
          <li><strong>Delivery state in alert history</strong>: History shows each notification target's result, and a retry sends again only to the targets that failed.</li>
          <li><strong>Straight to SSO</strong>: When SSO is the only sign-in method, the login page sends users straight to the identity provider. It still shows the page after a logout or a failed sign-in (<a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/issues/190" target="_blank">#190</a>).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type changed">Changed</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Explorer navigation</strong>: One owner handles navigation and the URL, and one handles query runs. A page load runs the query once, a connected source is picked first, a bare URL gets its query written back, and Back and Forward restore the right view. Window, zoom and limit changes rerun the last query that ran, not unrun editor text. An edited saved query stays selected and offers Update.</li>
          <li><strong>Links that cannot open</strong>: A link to a team you cannot access shows a message and runs nothing. A link to an unavailable source opens a fallback source, names both sources and does not run the linked query.</li>
          <li><strong>Faster queries and source lists</strong>: Queries no longer check the source live before each run, and source lists serve health from provider caches.</li>
          <li><strong>Configuration</strong>: Config file keys are normalized to lowercase before environment overrides apply, and keys that differ only in case are rejected. Local admin emails use the same validation as <code>admin_emails</code>. Non-empty frontend, external and AI base URLs must be absolute <code>http(s)</code> URLs with a host.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type security">Security</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>OIDC login</strong>: PKCE (S256), a nonce bound to a private cookie, bounded callback requests and rejection of padded email claims.</li>
          <li><strong>Admin rights</strong>: An API token with <code>users:write</code> can no longer grant admin. Role changes are serialized, so concurrent requests cannot demote or remove the last admin.</li>
          <li><strong>Queries</strong>: ClickHouse alert queries must be a single <code>SELECT</code> and run exactly as validated. Template variables are escaped for each query language.</li>
          <li><strong>Secrets</strong>: Logs no longer contain share tokens or credentials, API tokens cannot read secret settings, and error responses no longer expose backend details. The login rate limiter has bounded memory, and account emails are capped at 254 bytes.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type fixed">Fixed</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>UTC timestamps on SQLite</strong>, so session limits, expiry and alert schedules compare correctly.</li>
          <li><strong>Reliable alerts</strong>: Firing state is preserved, delivery retries are capped, interrupted deliveries are recovered, shutdown no longer records false evaluation errors, and duplicate legacy firings are pruned.</li>
          <li><strong>A hung ClickHouse server</strong> no longer stalls other queries and health checks. Exports are safe across replicas and restarts.</li>
          <li><strong>Provisioning</strong> no longer deadlocks with admin deletes, and duplicate team-source links and members are no-ops. Collections and their owners are created atomically.</li>
          <li><strong>Web UI</strong>: One toast per failure, not two. Late responses no longer overwrite newer state in alerts, the timeline, live tail, the save-query dialog and team settings. Sharing a query in SQL mode no longer fails with HTTP 400. Query errors return the correct HTTP status codes.</li>
          <li><strong>Query templates</strong>: Optional blocks are recognized by balanced brackets and a variable, using each language's string and comment syntax, and they work in streaming and background exports.</li>
          <li><strong>Alert evaluation and webhooks</strong>: Alert queries returning any ClickHouse integer or float width are evaluated. Webhook requests share one client with a timeout and bounded reads, and delivery history keeps the HTTP status without the response body. Stopping the server cancels running evaluations.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="version" id="v2.4.0">
      <div class="version-header">
        <a class="version-tag" href="#v2.4.0">v2.4.0</a>
        <span class="version-date">Oct 13, 2026</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/releases/tag/v2.4.0" class="version-link" target="_blank">Release notes &rarr;</a>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type added">Added</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>VictoriaLogs windowed search</strong>: Opt in per source to receive newest-first pages with streamed progress, parallel time windows, stream selection, and Stop, Skip, and Retry controls. Background counts show totals, histogram buckets, and coverage as windows finish. Retry failed counts in one action while keeping completed counts. Configure the feature in the source form or provisioning file. Read the <a href="/guide/windowed-search">windowed-search guide</a> for settings and limits (<a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/issues/116" target="_blank">#116</a>).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type changed">Changed</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li>Optimized sources discover schema fields from the last five minutes by default. Increase the schema lookback if fields appear rarely.</li>
          <li>Windowed pagination selects a timestamp boundary before sorting full rows, reducing work on dense windows. Searches started with an older cursor must be run again after upgrading.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type fixed">Fixed</div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li>A short page caused by an incorrect VictoriaLogs timestamp boundary is read again across the full window so older rows are not silently omitted.</li>
          <li>Search retries retain the next-window cursor and restore completion when no gaps remain. Query and histogram endpoints validate stream filters consistently and reject them for sources other than VictoriaLogs.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- v2.3.2 -->
    <article class="version" id="v2.3.2">
      <div class="version-header">
        <a class="version-tag" href="#v2.3.2">v2.3.2</a>
        <span class="version-date">Oct 8, 2026</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/releases/tag/v2.3.2" class="version-link" target="_blank">Release notes &rarr;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="release-intro">
        Shared collections can be shared with whole teams. Metadata migrations run automatically at startup: SQLite to version 34 and Postgres to version 9. Back up the metadata database before upgrading.
      </p>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type added">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg>
          Added
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Share a collection with a team</strong>: Owners choose <strong>Share with team</strong> to give every current team member the collection Member role. Membership is live: people who join the team see the collection, and people who leave lose it unless they have another share or a direct membership. A team share never grants Editor or Owner rights, and a direct role takes precedence. Owners can share with teams they belong to; global admins can share their own collections with any team. Owners can remove a share even after leaving the team (<a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/issues/114" target="_blank">#114</a>).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type changed">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 20v-8m0 0V4m0 8h8m-8 0H4"/></svg>
          Changed
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Source access</strong>: Team shares never grant source access. Running, pinning, and editing a query still need access to its source; other items show a lock icon. A collection shared through several teams and a direct membership appears once.</li>
          <li><strong>Collection counts</strong>: Collections report <code>team_count</code> beside <code>member_count</code>. <code>member_count</code> still counts direct members only, owners included.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type fixed">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg>
          Fixed
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li>SQLite alerts now evaluate at their configured interval instead of waiting for the cutoff date to change. Existing timestamps need no migration (<a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/issues/124" target="_blank">#124</a>).</li>
          <li>Helm deployments now preserve all configuration sections, use persistent credentials and Dex storage, and support configurable security contexts. Read the <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/blob/main/deployment/helm/README.md" target="_blank">chart upgrade guide</a> before upgrading.</li>
          <li>The MCP investigation panel applies each new call and runs its query. MCP tools now declare their read-only and closed-world behavior.</li>
          <li><strong>Collection detail</strong>: <code>GET /api/v1/collections/:id</code> returns the member and item counts instead of zero, so the detail and the list agree.</li>
          <li><strong>Collection header</strong> no longer shows counts twice, such as "3 3 items".</li>
          <li><strong>Removed from a team</strong>: Pages without a team in the URL, such as the Library, no longer open Access Denied after someone removes you from your last-used team. On load, Logchef checks the saved team against your current teams and switches to one you still belong to, or clears the selection if none remain.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- v2.3.1 -->
    <article class="version" id="v2.3.1">
      <div class="version-header">
        <a class="version-tag" href="#v2.3.1">v2.3.1</a>
        <span class="version-date">Oct 7, 2026</span>

        <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/releases/tag/v2.3.1" class="version-link" target="_blank">Release notes &rarr;</a>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type fixed">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg>
          Fixed
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li>OAuth token and revocation responses now carry <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>.</li>
          <li>A read-only demo instance can approve an OAuth connection and revoke a connected app; every other write stays blocked.</li>
          <li>Handlers no longer pass the HTTP server's pooled request context to database and datasource calls, which raced with server start and shutdown.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type changed">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 20v-8m0 0V4m0 8h8m-8 0H4"/></svg>
          Changed
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li>zitadel/oidc v3.51.13, which includes the upstream fix for its audience decoder panic.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- v2.3.0 -->
    <article class="version" id="v2.3.0">
      <div class="version-header">
        <a class="version-tag" href="#v2.3.0">v2.3.0</a>
        <span class="version-date">Oct 7, 2026</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/releases/tag/v2.3.0" class="version-link" target="_blank">Release notes &rarr;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="release-intro">
        Logchef 2.3 can now be its own OAuth server for agents and the CLI, and serves MCP at <code>/mcp</code> from the same process. Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and other MCP hosts connect with a browser approval instead of a pasted token. This release adds database migration 33; back up the metadata database before upgrading.
      </p>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type added">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg>
          Added
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Built-in MCP endpoint</strong>: With OAuth enabled, <code>&lt;server.public_url&gt;/mcp</code> serves read tools for profile, teams, sources, schema, LogchefQL and SQL queries, histograms, field values, log context, saved queries and alerts, plus <code>compare_windows</code>, <code>top_values</code> and an investigation panel for hosts that render MCP Apps. Tool calls use the same access checks and query limits as the HTTP API. The separate logchef-mcp server is no longer needed.</li>
          <li><strong>Logchef OAuth</strong>: Set <code>server.public_url</code> and <code>[auth.oauth] enabled = true</code> to let agents and the CLI sign in through a consent page after the normal Logchef login. Authorization code with S256 PKCE, rotating refresh tokens, and tokens bound to either the API or <code>/mcp</code>. Users review and revoke grants under Settings &rarr; Connected apps.</li>
          <li><strong>Built-in MCP clients</strong>: Claude Code, Codex and Cursor connect with the built-in client ID <code>logchef-mcp</code>; hosted web clients such as Claude.ai and ChatGPT are configured under <code>[[auth.oauth.clients]]</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Split UI and API hosts</strong>: <code>server.browser_url</code> puts the consent page on a UI host behind an SSO proxy while the CLI and agents use <code>server.public_url</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>CLI</strong>: <code>logchef agent setup</code> prints the steps to connect a coding agent.</li>
          <li><code>query.mcp_call_timeout_seconds</code> (default 60) bounds each MCP tool call.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type changed">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 20v-8m0 0V4m0 8h8m-8 0H4"/></svg>
          Changed
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>CLI sign-in</strong>: <code>logchef auth</code> signs in through Logchef OAuth and needs a server with OAuth enabled. <code>--no-browser</code> prints the URL, and <code>auth --logout</code> revokes the grant on the server. API tokens work as before.</li>
          <li>OAuth tokens get team membership access only, also for admins. Admin sessions and API tokens are unchanged.</li>
          <li>LogchefQL accepts RFC3339 and fractional-second time ranges on ClickHouse.</li>
          <li>Team and source access checks run in the server core for every caller. An unknown path under a source not linked to the team returns 404, and rate-limited routes report the limit before the link check.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type fixed">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg>
          Fixed
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li>A saved query or alert that does not exist or is not visible returned 200 with empty data, and alert history returned 500. They now return 404.</li>
          <li>Collection errors (403, 404, 409) were reported as 500.</li>
          <li>A ClickHouse server that accepted connections but never answered held every query for the full dial timeout. Queries now stop at their own deadline.</li>
          <li><code>logchef doctor</code> reported a saved token's expiry when <code>--token</code> overrode it.</li>
          <li>An invalid alert test query returns 400 instead of 500.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type removed">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M19 12H5"/></svg>
          Removed
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><code>POST /api/v1/cli/token</code> and the <code>oidc.cli_client_id</code> setting. Existing configs that still set <code>cli_client_id</code> load unchanged.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type breaking">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 9v4m0 4h.01M10.3 4.2L2.7 17.4A2 2 0 004.4 20h15.2a2 2 0 001.7-2.6L13.7 4.2a2 2 0 00-3.4 0z"/></svg>
          Upgrade notes
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li>Back up the metadata database: migration 33 adds the OAuth tables.</li>
          <li>Update the CLI to 0.3.0. Older CLIs cannot sign in to this server and report that <code>oidc.cli_client_id</code> is not set; ignore that message and upgrade. Their saved sessions keep working until they expire.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- v2.2.0 -->
    <article class="version" id="v2.2.0">
      <div class="version-header">
        <a class="version-tag" href="#v2.2.0">v2.2.0</a>
        <span class="version-date">Sep 29, 2026</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/releases/tag/v2.2.0" class="version-link" target="_blank">Release notes &rarr;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="release-intro">
        Logchef 2.2 can run under a subpath behind a reverse proxy and completes the Simplified Chinese interface for sources, access, admin, and library views. The server moved to Fiber v3. There are no database migrations.
      </p>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type added">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg>
          Added
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Serve under a subpath</strong>: Run Logchef at a URL such as <code>https://example.com/logchef/</code>. Set <code>server.frontend_url</code> to the public URL, including the subpath, and have the proxy remove the prefix. Operations &rarr; Reverse Proxy has nginx, Caddy, and Traefik examples (<a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/issues/108" target="_blank">#108</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>More translated views</strong>: Add Source, source management and inspection, Access, Admin settings, query activity, Library views, route titles, and shared widgets use translation keys, with Simplified Chinese translations. Other languages show English for these messages until they are translated (<a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/issues/103" target="_blank">#103</a>).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="change-section">
        <div class="change-type changed">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 20v-8m0 0V4m0 8h8m-8 0H4"/></svg>
          Changed
        </div>
        <ul class="change-list">
          <li><strong>Auth cookies</strong> use the path of <code>server.frontend_url</code>. Deployments at <code>/</code> are unchanged.</li>
          <li><strong>CLI</strong>: <code>logchef open</code> keeps the path of the server URL, so it works with a subpath.</li>
          <li><strong>Fiber v3</strong>: The HTTP server moved from Fiber v2 to Fiber v3, and all Go, frontend, docs, and CLI dependencies are on their latest releases. Building from source requires Go 1.27.</li>
          <li><strong>Docker images</strong> use Alpine 3.24.2.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- v2.1.0 -->
    <article class="version milestone" id="v2.1.0">
      <div class="version-header">
        <a class="version-tag" href="#v2.1.0">v2.1.0</a>
        <span class="version-date">Sep 24, 2026</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/mr-karan/logchef/releases/tag/v2.1.0" class="version-link" target="_blank">Release notes &rarr;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="release-kicker">Fast at any width</p>
      <p class="release-intro">
        <strong>Logchef 2.1 makes the Explorer usable on sources with hundreds or thousands of fields</strong>, adds eleven interface languages, and ships a Helm chart. On a 3,000-field VictoriaLogs source, first rows appear in 1.6 seconds instead of 42, and 1,000 rows with every column shown use 73 MB instead of 1.8 GB. There are no database migrations.
      </p>

      <ul class="release-highlights">
        <li><strong>Fast on wide schemas</strong>The table, Fields sidebar, and Group by no longer render every field. Switching to 1,000 rows per page takes 0.4 s instead of 17 s.</li>
        <li><strong>Eleven languages</strong>English plus Chinese (Simplified and Traditional), Spanish, French, German, Portuguese, Japanese, Korean, Hindi, and Italian, saved to your account.</li>
        <li><strong>Helm chart</strong>Deploy Logchef with Dex and ClickHouse, with optional Ingress or Gateway API routes.</li>
        <li><strong>Exact values</strong>Large integers, precise decimals, and nanosecond timestamps are no longer rounded.</li>
        <li><strong>Accurate query stats</strong>"Rows read" reports what ClickHouse scanned, and <code>logchef_query_total</code> counts each query once.</li>
        <li><strong>VictoriaLogs alerts</strong>Alert queries read the metric value instead of a label, so Test Query works again.</li>
      </ul>

      <aside class="upgrade-note">
        <strong>Before upgrading:</strong> there are no database migrations. VictoriaLogs column visibility saved by older versions resets once to the six-column default, keeping order and widths. LogchefQL now requires quoting numbers compared with string fields, and <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, or <code>~</code> with <code>null</code> is an error. <a href="#v2-1-upgrade">All upgrade notes &darr;</a>
      </aside>

      <div class="change-section">
